15:08:13 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You're pointing at something real. The theory of monetization strategies and the actual lived reality for most apps are two very different things. Let me be more straight about it: ## The Ugly Truth **Most free apps don't make meaningful money, period.** - The vast majority of apps on any store earn **effectively $0** beyond covering server costs, if that - App store data consistently shows something like the top 1-5% of apps capture the vast majority of all revenue - Most developers are hobbyists, students, or people building portfolio pieces—not sustainable businesses **Why ads feel broken/useless:** - Ad revenue is contingent on *scale*. You need hundreds of thousands or millions of active users before ad pennies add up to real money - CPMs (cost per 1000 impressions) are often just $0.50–$5, so unless you have massive traffic, this is background noise, not income - Many small developers slap in ads more as a "why not" afterthought than a real strategy, and it shows **Why apps get abandoned:** - Maintenance is expensive (developer time, server costs, OS updates breaking things) - If the revenue isn't materializing, there's no incentive to keep updating - Many apps are side projects that served their purpose (learning, resume-building, solving a personal problem) and were never meant to be long-term businesses ## The survivorship bias problem When people explain "how apps make money," they're usually implicitly talking about the **outliers**—the Spotifys, the Candy Crushes, the apps that actually broke through. Those success stories get held up as *the model*, but they represent a tiny fraction of what's actually out there. ## The realistic picture For every polished app with smart monetization, there are thousands of: - Apps built by one person testing an idea - Apps that got moderate downloads but never found a revenue path - Apps kept alive out of stubbornness or attachment, not profit - Apps that are quietly funded by something else (a day job, a small patreon, unrelated business revenue) So your skepticism is well-founded. "Free apps make money through ads/freemium/etc." is true as a *category of options*, but it dramatically understates how rare it is for any given free app to actually generate real income this way.
