15:09:04 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Yeah, that's a fair summary of the mechanics. Scale is the multiplier that turns "technically a monetization strategy" into "actual money," and most apps never get anywhere close to the scale required. A couple of nuances worth adding, though not to walk back your point, just to sharpen it: **"No scale, no money" isn't quite absolute—there's a middle tier** It's not just binary (viral hit vs. worthless). There's a real but unglamorous middle ground: niche apps with a few thousand loyal users paying $5/month can generate enough to pay one person's rent. Not fortune, but not nothing either. These don't get talked about because they're boring—no one writes articles about "app makes founder $30k/year forever." **The theory isn't wrong, it's just incomplete without the caveat** The categories (ads, freemium, subscriptions, etc.) aren't fictional—they do work. But they work like fishing techniques: technically valid, but the technique isn't what catches the fish. Traffic/distribution/marketing is what catches the fish. Most "how do apps make money" explanations skip that part entirely, which is exactly the disconnect you're noticing. **The real bottleneck is distribution, not monetization** If you already have millions of users, almost any monetization method works fine. If you don't, no method works well. So in a sense, you've identified the actual causal chain: **acquiring/keeping large audiences is the hard, rare thing; picking a way to monetize them is comparatively trivial.** People just love talking about the trivial part because the hard part (marketing, virality, retention) is less tidy to explain in a listicle. So: not wrong to be cynical. The strategies are real, but as descriptions of common outcomes for average apps, they're closer to marketing mythology than typical experience.
